WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.063
Welcome to part three, the last one.

2
00:00:03.113 --> 00:00:06.393
We cover digital data transmission
and coding, analogue to

3
00:00:06.433 --> 00:00:09.541
digital conversion, radio
propagation and polarization,

4
00:00:09.581 --> 00:00:12.804
and antennas with the Friis
transmission equation, which

5
00:00:12.844 --> 00:00:18.231
gave the link-budget problems
of the 2022 and 2023 partials.

6
00:00:18.281 --> 00:00:21.383
As before, every exam
question on these topics is

7
00:00:21.423 --> 00:00:25.748
solved, and checked against the
official key where one exists.

8
00:00:27.398 --> 00:00:30.446
Data communication moves
characters from one point

9
00:00:30.486 --> 00:00:33.924
to another, today almost
always between computers.

10
00:00:33.974 --> 00:00:37.764
In parallel transmission every
bit of a character travels at the

11
00:00:37.804 --> 00:00:41.654
same time on its own line, which
is fast but needs many lines, so

12
00:00:41.694 --> 00:00:45.485
it is only used over very short
distances; the handwritten notes

13
00:00:45.525 --> 00:00:50.184
show the historical example of Salvà's
telegraph, one wire per letter.

14
00:00:50.234 --> 00:00:54.600
In serial transmission the bits travel
one after another on a single line,

15
00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:59.396
which takes longer but is the only
practical solution over long distances.

16
00:00:59.446 --> 00:01:03.640
A serial link must tell the receiver
where each word starts and ends: in

17
00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:07.875
asynchronous transmission every
character carries its own start and stop

18
00:01:07.915 --> 00:01:11.699
symbols, as drawn here, so the
circuits are simple, but the extra

19
00:01:11.739 --> 00:01:16.676
symbols carry no user information and
the gap between characters is not fixed.

20
00:01:16.726 --> 00:01:20.886
In synchronous transmission there are
no start or stop symbols: a special

21
00:01:20.926 --> 00:01:25.373
sequence synchronises the receiver's
clock once, and then a continuous flow of

22
00:01:25.413 --> 00:01:29.802
symbols is kept even when there is nothing
to send, which is faster but needs

23
00:01:29.842 --> 00:01:34.909
more sophisticated equipment, as in the
digital links between telephone exchanges.

24
00:01:36.559 --> 00:01:39.624
A binary signal takes one
of two values in each

25
00:01:39.664 --> 00:01:43.515
symbol interval, and an M-ary
signal one of M values.

26
00:01:43.565 --> 00:01:47.015
Nothing forces data transmission
to be binary: with four

27
00:01:47.055 --> 00:01:50.193
voltage levels, each level
carries two bits, and in

28
00:01:50.233 --> 00:01:54.446
general M symbols represent n
bits when M equals 2 to the n.

29
00:01:54.496 --> 00:01:58.608
In the figure, the binary signal
needs twelve symbols to send twelve

30
00:01:58.648 --> 00:02:03.126
bits, while the 8-ary signal sends
the same twelve bits with only four

31
00:02:03.166 --> 00:02:08.278
symbols, three bits each, so it is three
times faster at the same symbol rate.

32
00:02:08.328 --> 00:02:12.894
Problem 3.8 is the inverse question:
to code the five vowels you need at

33
00:02:12.934 --> 00:02:17.676
least 3 bits, because 2 bits only give
four words and 3 bits give eight.

34
00:02:19.326 --> 00:02:22.672
Do not confuse the two rates,
it is a favourite trap.

35
00:02:22.722 --> 00:02:26.128
The signalling rate is the
number of symbols per second,

36
00:02:26.168 --> 00:02:29.575
measured in baud, and a channel
of bandwidth B allows at

37
00:02:29.615 --> 00:02:33.904
most 2 B baud, a limit only
reached with special pulse shapes.

38
00:02:33.954 --> 00:02:38.631
The information rate is the number of
bits per second, which equals the baud

39
00:02:38.671 --> 00:02:43.986
rate times the number of bits per symbol,
n, where M equals 2 to the n levels.

40
00:02:44.036 --> 00:02:46.968
The two coincide only in a
binary synchronous link; in

41
00:02:47.008 --> 00:02:49.884
a binary asynchronous link
the bit rate is lower than

42
00:02:49.924 --> 00:02:52.745
the baud rate because of
the start and stop symbols;

43
00:02:52.785 --> 00:02:56.217
and in an M-ary synchronous
link the bit rate is higher.

44
00:02:56.267 --> 00:02:59.463
That is how old telephone
modems worked: half a telephone

45
00:02:59.503 --> 00:03:04.574
channel, about 1.5 kHz, allows
3 000 baud, yet the V.34

46
00:03:04.614 --> 00:03:10.131
standard reached 33 600 bits per
second with multilevel symbols.

47
00:03:11.781 --> 00:03:14.273
This problem, from the
collection, came back in

48
00:03:14.313 --> 00:03:19.782
2023 with the new 8.33 kHz
channels, so let us do both.

49
00:03:19.832 --> 00:03:26.242
The maximum signalling rate is twice the
bandwidth: 2 times 25 kHz, 50 kilobaud.

50
00:03:26.292 --> 00:03:30.248
With 4 levels each symbol carries
2 bits, because 2 squared

51
00:03:30.288 --> 00:03:34.667
is 4, so the information rate
is 50 thousand times 2, 100

52
00:03:34.707 --> 00:03:38.810
kilobits per second; Barlabé
confirms this step in the FAQ.

53
00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:43.955
With 30 dB, that is a ratio of
1 000, Shannon gives 25 000

54
00:03:43.995 --> 00:03:50.692
times log base 2 of 1 001, which
is 249.2 kilobits per second.

55
00:03:50.742 --> 00:03:57.953
For the 2023 version, the 8.333
kHz channel allows 16.67 kilobaud.

56
00:03:58.003 --> 00:04:01.408
Eight symbols carry 3 bits
each, so 50 kilobits per

57
00:04:01.448 --> 00:04:07.142
second, and the capacity at 30
dB is 83.1 kilobits per second.

58
00:04:08.792 --> 00:04:14.401
If multilevel signals carry more bits per
symbol, why not use thousands of levels?

59
00:04:14.451 --> 00:04:18.581
Because, for the same signal power,
more levels means the levels

60
00:04:18.621 --> 00:04:22.816
are closer together, and noise
pushes the received value across a

61
00:04:22.856 --> 00:04:27.375
decision threshold more easily,
as the handwritten notes sketch.

62
00:04:27.425 --> 00:04:30.376
These curves show it: at
any given signal to noise

63
00:04:30.416 --> 00:04:34.416
ratio, the error probability
grows with the number of levels.

64
00:04:34.466 --> 00:04:38.272
So in the 2016 partial, with
the same signal to noise

65
00:04:38.312 --> 00:04:41.508
ratio and the same signalling
rate, the 16-level

66
00:04:41.548 --> 00:04:45.439
signal has a larger error
probability than the binary one.

67
00:04:45.489 --> 00:04:49.949
In the presence of noise you cannot make
a digital signal look more and more

68
00:04:49.989 --> 00:04:54.779
like an analog one; coding is then used
to recover part of the reliability.

69
00:04:56.429 --> 00:04:59.332
Modulation is processing
of the signal; coding is

70
00:04:59.372 --> 00:05:02.665
processing of the symbols
of the digital message.

71
00:05:02.715 --> 00:05:05.994
Source coding removes redundancy
so that the source is

72
00:05:06.034 --> 00:05:09.190
represented with fewer bits:
Morse does it by giving

73
00:05:09.230 --> 00:05:12.448
the shortest words to the
most frequent letters, like

74
00:05:12.488 --> 00:05:15.767
a single dot for E, compressors
like ZIP do it without

75
00:05:15.807 --> 00:05:18.656
loss, and JPEG, MPEG or MP3 do it with an

76
00:05:18.696 --> 00:05:21.730
imperceptible loss; reducing
the number of bits is

77
00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:26.668
exactly the 'main objective of source
coding' asked in the question sheet.

78
00:05:26.718 --> 00:05:30.918
Channel coding does the opposite:
it adds bits to detect and correct

79
00:05:30.958 --> 00:05:35.158
transmission errors and to keep
synchronism, with parity bits, block

80
00:05:35.198 --> 00:05:40.536
codes, Hamming codes and convolutional
codes decoded with the Viterbi algorithm.

81
00:05:40.586 --> 00:05:45.533
A single parity bit detects one
error, misses two, and corrects none.

82
00:05:45.583 --> 00:05:49.802
The Hamming code of the notes maps
4 data bits into 7, choosing 16

83
00:05:49.842 --> 00:05:54.770
of the 128 possible words so that
any two differ in at least three

84
00:05:54.810 --> 00:05:58.100
bits, so one or two errors are
detected and a single error is

85
00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:02.257
corrected by picking the closest
valid word; the price is redundancy.

86
00:06:02.307 --> 00:06:05.938
For the exam, 'name a source
code and a channel code' has a

87
00:06:05.978 --> 00:06:10.621
ready answer: Morse or ZIP for source,
parity or Hamming for channel.

88
00:06:12.271 --> 00:06:17.358
The last family of channel codes shapes
the electrical waveform: line codes.

89
00:06:17.408 --> 00:06:21.683
Binary signals can be unipolar
or bipolar, and return to zero,

90
00:06:21.723 --> 00:06:25.789
R Z, if they go back to zero
during half of the bit, or non

91
00:06:25.829 --> 00:06:30.634
return to zero, N R Z, if they
hold the level for the whole bit.

92
00:06:30.684 --> 00:06:34.813
A long run of zeros in unipolar
format has no transitions, so the

93
00:06:34.853 --> 00:06:38.855
receiver loses bit synchronism,
and a long run of ones leaves a

94
00:06:38.895 --> 00:06:43.735
non-zero mean, a DC component, which
transmission systems must avoid.

95
00:06:43.785 --> 00:06:47.850
A M I, alternate mark inversion,
makes successive ones alternate

96
00:06:47.890 --> 00:06:51.892
in polarity, which removes the
DC component, but a long run of

97
00:06:51.932 --> 00:06:55.997
zeros is still a flat line with
no transitions; H D B three, not

98
00:06:56.037 --> 00:07:00.102
drawn here, fixes that by replacing
every run of four zeros with

99
00:07:00.142 --> 00:07:04.534
a pattern containing pulses, so
the receiver keeps synchronism.

100
00:07:04.584 --> 00:07:07.784
Local networks use the Manchester
code, with a guaranteed

101
00:07:07.824 --> 00:07:10.855
transition in the middle of
every bit: no DC and easy

102
00:07:10.895 --> 00:07:14.883
synchronism, but at the cost of
a larger transmission bandwidth.

103
00:07:16.533 --> 00:07:19.800
Long-distance data links
modulate a sinusoidal

104
00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:23.035
carrier, and with a
digital modulating signal

105
00:07:23.075 --> 00:07:28.888
amplitude, frequency and phase
modulation are called ASK, FSK and PSK.

106
00:07:28.938 --> 00:07:33.486
Facing noise, for a given signal
to noise ratio, PSK gives the

107
00:07:33.526 --> 00:07:38.750
lowest error probability, followed
by FSK, and ASK is the worst.

108
00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:42.370
A modem, short for modulator-demodulator,
is the equipment that

109
00:07:42.410 --> 00:07:46.151
converts a low-pass digital signal
into a band-pass digital signal

110
00:07:46.191 --> 00:07:51.468
and back, which is the answer to 'what
is a modem' in the 2023 partial.

111
00:07:51.518 --> 00:07:55.209
To go faster, modems combine
amplitude and phase, and such

112
00:07:55.249 --> 00:07:59.068
multilevel signals are drawn as
a constellation: one dot per

113
00:07:59.108 --> 00:08:02.992
symbol at the tip of its phasor,
here twelve phases, the four

114
00:08:03.032 --> 00:08:07.948
inner points sharing four of them,
and two amplitudes: sixteen symbols.

115
00:08:07.998 --> 00:08:11.439
Above the line codes,
communication protocols, organised

116
00:08:11.479 --> 00:08:14.919
in the seven layers of the I
S O O S I model, decide who

117
00:08:14.959 --> 00:08:18.913
talks, how errors are reported
and how packets are routed.

118
00:08:20.563 --> 00:08:23.533
Analogue to digital conversion
is the last topic of

119
00:08:23.573 --> 00:08:27.287
the exam scope, and its block
diagram is a sure question.

120
00:08:27.337 --> 00:08:31.578
First comes the anti-aliasing
filter, an analogue low-pass filter

121
00:08:31.618 --> 00:08:35.662
that guarantees a signal band-limited
to W at the input of the

122
00:08:35.702 --> 00:08:41.190
sampler; that is why a filter is needed
as the first stage, to avoid aliasing.

123
00:08:41.240 --> 00:08:44.511
Then the sampler measures the
signal at regular instants

124
00:08:44.551 --> 00:08:48.625
separated by the sampling period
T s, giving a discrete signal.

125
00:08:48.675 --> 00:08:52.123
Then the quantizer assigns to
each sample the nearest of a

126
00:08:52.163 --> 00:08:56.723
finite number of amplitude levels,
giving a multilevel digital signal.

127
00:08:56.773 --> 00:09:00.160
Finally the coder replaces
each level by an N-bit binary

128
00:09:00.200 --> 00:09:04.100
word, so the bit time is the
sampling period divided by N.

129
00:09:04.150 --> 00:09:09.693
Filter, sampler, quantizer, coder:
memorise the four names in this order, in

130
00:09:09.733 --> 00:09:16.180
Catalan if you like: filtre anti-aliasing,
mostrejador, quantificador, codificador.

131
00:09:17.830 --> 00:09:21.721
Why must the sampling frequency
be at least twice the bandwidth?

132
00:09:21.771 --> 00:09:27.188
The spectrum of a sampled signal is
periodic: the original spectrum, of width

133
00:09:27.228 --> 00:09:32.965
W on each side, is repeated around every
multiple of the sampling frequency.

134
00:09:33.015 --> 00:09:36.989
As long as f s is at least twice
W, the copies do not touch,

135
00:09:37.029 --> 00:09:41.527
and an ideal low-pass filter can
recover the original exactly.

136
00:09:41.577 --> 00:09:45.911
Watch what happens as the sampling
frequency is lowered: below twice W

137
00:09:45.951 --> 00:09:49.973
the replicas overlap, and the sum
in red no longer looks like the

138
00:09:50.013 --> 00:09:54.988
original; this is aliasing, a distortion
that nothing can undo afterwards.

139
00:09:55.038 --> 00:09:58.848
The minimum, twice W, is the
Nyquist frequency, and because

140
00:09:58.888 --> 00:10:02.762
real filters need a transition
band we sample a little faster:

141
00:10:02.802 --> 00:10:09.401
the compact disc samples 20 kHz
audio at 44.1 kHz instead of 40.

142
00:10:11.051 --> 00:10:14.763
The quantizer is a non-linear
system, so it has no transfer

143
00:10:14.803 --> 00:10:19.667
function; it is described by its
staircase input-output characteristic.

144
00:10:19.717 --> 00:10:24.357
Its error, the difference between each
sample and the nearest level, cannot be

145
00:10:24.397 --> 00:10:29.367
corrected when converting back, but it
becomes negligible with enough levels.

146
00:10:29.417 --> 00:10:36.049
Voice, in telephony or between pilot and
controller, needs 256 levels, 8 bits,

147
00:10:36.089 --> 00:10:44.651
while high-quality music on a CD uses 65
536 levels, 16 bits, per stereo channel.

148
00:10:44.701 --> 00:10:47.964
The quantization steps need
not be equal: non-uniform

149
00:10:48.004 --> 00:10:52.841
quantizers use smaller steps for the
most probable values of the signal.

150
00:10:52.891 --> 00:10:57.186
After coding, each sample becomes N
bits, so the binary signal is N times

151
00:10:57.226 --> 00:11:01.522
faster than the multilevel one, and
the converter is characterised by its

152
00:11:01.562 --> 00:11:06.128
number of bits per sample together
with its maximum sampling frequency.

153
00:11:07.778 --> 00:11:12.494
Here is the numerical example of
the notes, a perfect exam question.

154
00:11:12.544 --> 00:11:16.811
Sampling at 8 kHz gives
one sample every 125

155
00:11:16.851 --> 00:11:21.508
microseconds, and 256 levels
need 8 bits per sample.

156
00:11:21.558 --> 00:11:25.984
Eight bits must fit in
125 microseconds, so each

157
00:11:26.024 --> 00:11:31.521
bit lasts 15.625 microseconds,
giving 64 000 baud.

158
00:11:31.571 --> 00:11:34.904
In a binary synchronous link
the bit rate equals the baud

159
00:11:34.944 --> 00:11:39.317
rate: 64 kilobits per second,
the classic telephone channel.

160
00:11:39.367 --> 00:11:43.084
And since a channel of bandwidth
B carries at most 2 B baud, the

161
00:11:43.124 --> 00:11:47.924
minimum bandwidth is 32 kHz, far
more than the 8 kHz that the

162
00:11:47.964 --> 00:11:51.510
notes quote for analog transmission:
digital pays in bandwidth,

163
00:11:51.550 --> 00:11:56.284
which is why compression algorithms
such as M P 3 or JPEG were developed.

164
00:11:57.934 --> 00:12:01.291
Digital to analogue
conversion is much simpler.

165
00:12:01.341 --> 00:12:04.725
A decoder turns the binary
words back into a multilevel

166
00:12:04.765 --> 00:12:07.837
signal, and a low-pass
filter removes the spectral

167
00:12:07.877 --> 00:12:12.024
replicas created by sampling,
leaving only the original band.

168
00:12:12.074 --> 00:12:17.567
If the sampling respected Nyquist, the
recovery is perfect, at least in theory.

169
00:12:17.617 --> 00:12:20.822
The only difference between
the original and the recovered

170
00:12:20.862 --> 00:12:25.073
signal is the small quantization
error introduced in the A to D step.

171
00:12:26.723 --> 00:12:30.799
Now propagation, starting with the
plane wave, the simplest solution of

172
00:12:30.839 --> 00:12:35.594
Maxwell's equations and a good local
model of any wave far from its antenna.

173
00:12:35.644 --> 00:12:40.246
In a plane wave the electric field, the
magnetic field and the direction of

174
00:12:40.286 --> 00:12:44.949
propagation are mutually perpendicular,
and the ratio between E and H is the

175
00:12:44.989 --> 00:12:49.652
intrinsic impedance of the medium, the
square root of mu over epsilon, about

176
00:12:49.692 --> 00:12:56.416
377 ohms in vacuum, the analogue of the
characteristic impedance of a line.

177
00:12:56.466 --> 00:12:59.618
The wave number is omega over
the propagation speed, which

178
00:12:59.658 --> 00:13:02.370
is one over the square root
of mu epsilon, and the

179
00:13:02.410 --> 00:13:06.336
refractive index is the square
root of the relative permittivity.

180
00:13:06.386 --> 00:13:10.381
At a discontinuity, part of the wave
is reflected, with the angle of

181
00:13:10.421 --> 00:13:14.238
reflection equal to the angle of
incidence, and part is refracted

182
00:13:14.278 --> 00:13:18.451
into the second medium following
Snell's law, n 1 sine theta i equals

183
00:13:18.491 --> 00:13:23.350
n 2 sine theta t, with all angles
measured from the normal to the surface.

184
00:13:23.400 --> 00:13:27.331
The reflection coefficient depends
on both media, on the angle of

185
00:13:27.371 --> 00:13:31.607
incidence and on the polarization:
decompose the electric field into a

186
00:13:31.647 --> 00:13:35.821
component perpendicular to the plane
of incidence and one parallel to

187
00:13:35.861 --> 00:13:40.097
it; for a reflection from the ground,
a horizontally polarized wave is

188
00:13:40.137 --> 00:13:44.190
the perpendicular case and a
vertically polarized wave the parallel

189
00:13:44.230 --> 00:13:48.221
one; each has its own reflection
coefficient, so an oblique linear

190
00:13:48.261 --> 00:13:53.681
polarization generally comes back with a
different orientation, or even elliptical.

191
00:13:53.731 --> 00:13:57.022
Diffraction is what cannot be
explained by reflection or

192
00:13:57.062 --> 00:14:01.067
refraction: by Huygens' principle
every point of a front radiates a

193
00:14:01.107 --> 00:14:06.097
new wave, so waves go round obstacles,
which gives some coverage behind hills.

194
00:14:07.747 --> 00:14:11.522
The main propagation mechanism
depends on the frequency band.

195
00:14:11.572 --> 00:14:16.324
Below about 100 kHz, waves are
guided between the Earth's surface

196
00:14:16.364 --> 00:14:19.964
and the ionosphere; this was
used by the old Omega and Loran

197
00:14:20.004 --> 00:14:24.600
navigation systems, and today only
for communications with submarines.

198
00:14:24.650 --> 00:14:30.194
Between 100 kHz and 3 MHz the surface
wave dominates, following the

199
00:14:30.234 --> 00:14:34.612
ground; it needs vertical polarization,
which is why the NDB and ADF

200
00:14:34.652 --> 00:14:39.849
system uses vertically polarized antennas,
and AM broadcasting lives here too.

201
00:14:39.899 --> 00:14:44.681
Between 3 and 30 MHz, waves bounce
off the ionosphere, reaching

202
00:14:44.721 --> 00:14:49.328
very long distances, farther at
night because the lower layer rises.

203
00:14:49.378 --> 00:14:54.403
Above 30 MHz propagation is optical,
along the line of sight, with a

204
00:14:54.443 --> 00:14:58.753
direct wave and a ground-reflected
wave in the troposphere: this is

205
00:14:58.793 --> 00:15:03.558
where aeronautical VHF voice, VOR,
DME, TACAN, secondary radar and

206
00:15:03.598 --> 00:15:08.688
satellite systems work, and it is the
regime for all the Friis problems.

207
00:15:10.338 --> 00:15:14.209
Polarization is the orientation in
space of the electric field of the

208
00:15:14.249 --> 00:15:19.418
wave, the definition asked word for
word in 2016 and in the question sheet.

209
00:15:19.468 --> 00:15:23.086
To see it, fix a point, for
example z equal to zero, and

210
00:15:23.126 --> 00:15:27.330
follow the tip of the electric
field vector as time passes.

211
00:15:27.380 --> 00:15:32.384
If the field has a single component,
or two components in phase, the tip

212
00:15:32.424 --> 00:15:37.889
moves along a line: linear polarization,
vertical, horizontal or oblique.

213
00:15:37.939 --> 00:15:41.723
If the two components are 90
degrees apart, the tip draws an

214
00:15:41.763 --> 00:15:45.638
ellipse, and if they are also
equal in amplitude, a circle.

215
00:15:45.688 --> 00:15:49.804
Watch the four cases:
vertical, oblique x plus 2 y,

216
00:15:49.844 --> 00:15:54.350
circular x minus j y, and
elliptical 2 x minus j y.

217
00:15:54.400 --> 00:15:59.370
Antennas take the name of the polarization
they radiate far away: a vertical dipole

218
00:15:59.410 --> 00:16:04.770
is vertically polarized, and aeronautical
voice antennas use vertical polarization.

219
00:16:06.420 --> 00:16:11.754
Barlabé's method, from the FAQ: fix z
equal to zero, write any j as e to the j

220
00:16:11.794 --> 00:16:17.718
90 degrees, take the real part, and look
at how each component varies with time.

221
00:16:17.768 --> 00:16:22.321
In part a, the x component
is 2 E zero cosine omega t.

222
00:16:22.371 --> 00:16:26.558
The y component has a j in front,
so it is 2 E zero cosine of

223
00:16:26.598 --> 00:16:31.510
omega t plus 90 degrees, which
is minus 2 E zero sine omega t.

224
00:16:31.560 --> 00:16:36.105
Equal amplitudes and a 90 degree
shift: the tip of E draws a circle,

225
00:16:36.145 --> 00:16:40.954
so the polarization is circular,
rotating from plus x towards minus y.

226
00:16:41.004 --> 00:16:43.672
The answer key calls
it left-hand circular,

227
00:16:43.712 --> 00:16:46.820
consistent with the notes'
worked example, where x

228
00:16:46.860 --> 00:16:50.862
minus j y, rotating the other
way, is right-hand circular.

229
00:16:50.912 --> 00:16:54.755
In part b both components are
j times E zero, so both equal

230
00:16:54.795 --> 00:16:58.638
minus E zero sine omega t: they
are in phase, the tip moves

231
00:16:58.678 --> 00:17:04.161
along the 45 degree diagonal, and
the polarization is linear, oblique.

232
00:17:05.811 --> 00:17:09.302
An antenna is the transition
region, the interface, between

233
00:17:09.342 --> 00:17:12.653
a wave guided by a transmission
line and a wave radiated

234
00:17:12.693 --> 00:17:16.184
into space, and it can also
give the radiation a direction;

235
00:17:16.224 --> 00:17:20.465
opening up the end of a two-wire
line is how the dipole was born.

236
00:17:20.515 --> 00:17:24.074
Far from the antenna, in the
far-field or Fraunhofer zone, the

237
00:17:24.114 --> 00:17:28.353
wave arriving at a receiver can be
treated locally as a plane wave.

238
00:17:28.403 --> 00:17:32.453
Do not confuse isotropic and
omnidirectional: an isotropic radiator

239
00:17:32.493 --> 00:17:36.909
radiates equally in every direction
of space and cannot physically exist,

240
00:17:36.949 --> 00:17:42.244
while an omnidirectional antenna radiates
equally in all directions of one plane.

241
00:17:42.294 --> 00:17:45.384
Each system needs its
antenna: broadcasting uses an

242
00:17:45.424 --> 00:17:48.575
omnidirectional transmitter
and directive receivers,

243
00:17:48.615 --> 00:17:51.276
or omnidirectional ones
if there are several

244
00:17:51.316 --> 00:17:54.283
transmitters; a point to
point link uses two very

245
00:17:54.323 --> 00:17:58.724
directive antennas; and a radar
uses a rotating directive antenna.

246
00:17:58.774 --> 00:18:02.498
By Lorentz reciprocity, the same
antenna transmits and receives

247
00:18:02.538 --> 00:18:06.891
with the same pattern, so we simply
speak of the radiation pattern.

248
00:18:06.941 --> 00:18:11.660
So the 2022 question about the pattern
of an aeronautical voice antenna on

249
00:18:11.700 --> 00:18:15.435
an aircraft has this answer:
omnidirectional in the horizontal plane,

250
00:18:15.475 --> 00:18:20.201
because the ground stations can be in any
direction, with vertical polarization.

251
00:18:21.851 --> 00:18:25.333
Barlabé's slides on antenna
structures are a picture gallery,

252
00:18:25.373 --> 00:18:29.190
so for the exam just know the
families and where they are used.

253
00:18:29.240 --> 00:18:33.370
Dipoles, usually half a wavelength
long, and their arrays, as in

254
00:18:33.410 --> 00:18:37.801
the ILS localizer; folded dipoles;
and quarter-wave monopoles over

255
00:18:37.841 --> 00:18:42.686
a ground plane, sometimes with a
capacitive load or inside a fairing.

256
00:18:42.736 --> 00:18:47.557
Slots and slot arrays, common in radars,
travelling-wave antennas, horns,

257
00:18:47.597 --> 00:18:53.140
and parabolic reflectors such as the
Cassegrain, the offset and the Gregorian.

258
00:18:53.190 --> 00:18:57.370
Helical antennas, Yagi-Uda
arrays, microstrip patches like

259
00:18:57.410 --> 00:19:02.098
those of the SMOS satellite,
lenses, and wideband designs such

260
00:19:02.138 --> 00:19:07.507
as the biconical, the discone, the
log-periodic and fractal antennas.

261
00:19:07.557 --> 00:19:11.234
On aircraft, the NAV COM antennas
are monopoles or dipoles, inside

262
00:19:11.274 --> 00:19:14.951
fairings on fast aircraft, which
is exactly your course project: a

263
00:19:14.991 --> 00:19:18.555
vertical quarter-wave monopole or
half-wave dipole for the voice

264
00:19:18.595 --> 00:19:27.510
band, 118 to 137 MHz, centred at
127.5 MHz, where the wavelength is

265
00:19:27.550 --> 00:19:34.320
2.35 m, so about 59 centimetres or
1.18 metres before end corrections.

266
00:19:35.970 --> 00:19:40.302
Now the most important formula of
the partial, derived in four steps.

267
00:19:40.352 --> 00:19:44.706
An isotropic radiator in a lossless
medium spreads its power P T over a

268
00:19:44.746 --> 00:19:49.224
sphere, so at distance R the power
density is P T over 4 pi R squared,

269
00:19:49.264 --> 00:19:54.936
falling with the square of the distance:
6 dB every time the distance doubles.

270
00:19:54.986 --> 00:19:59.101
A real antenna concentrates power
in some directions; the directivity

271
00:19:59.141 --> 00:20:03.315
function D of theta and phi is the
ratio between its power density and

272
00:20:03.355 --> 00:20:08.281
the isotropic one, so the density towards
the receiver is multiplied by D T.

273
00:20:08.331 --> 00:20:12.360
The receiving antenna behaves like
a funnel: the power it delivers

274
00:20:12.400 --> 00:20:16.820
to its load is the incident density
times its effective area A ef.

275
00:20:16.870 --> 00:20:19.986
Reciprocity links the two
parameters: the effective

276
00:20:20.026 --> 00:20:23.965
area is lambda squared over
4 pi times the directivity.

277
00:20:24.015 --> 00:20:27.785
Putting it together: P L
equals P T times D T times

278
00:20:27.825 --> 00:20:31.519
D R times lambda over 4
pi R squared, times the

279
00:20:31.559 --> 00:20:35.329
product of loss coefficients
c i, each between zero

280
00:20:35.369 --> 00:20:39.063
and one, for medium losses,
polarization mismatch,

281
00:20:39.103 --> 00:20:42.723
mispointing, mismatch and
efficiency; and, as the

282
00:20:42.763 --> 00:20:47.446
FAQ insists, everything in
linear units, watts and metres.

283
00:20:49.096 --> 00:20:53.173
The notes use this example to
show the orders of magnitude.

284
00:20:53.223 --> 00:20:56.379
Convert the directivity:
3 dB is a factor of

285
00:20:56.419 --> 00:20:59.610
2, and the distance is
ten to the 4 metres.

286
00:20:59.660 --> 00:21:03.555
Power density times effective
area: 1 000 watts over 4

287
00:21:03.595 --> 00:21:07.696
pi times ten to the 8, times
2, times one square metre.

288
00:21:07.746 --> 00:21:11.056
That gives only 1.59 microwatts.

289
00:21:11.106 --> 00:21:16.036
From one kilowatt to one and a half
microwatts is an attenuation of 88 dB,

290
00:21:16.076 --> 00:21:20.732
which is why received radio powers are
always tiny and are expressed in dBm.

291
00:21:22.382 --> 00:21:25.601
The radiation pattern draws
the power density radiated

292
00:21:25.641 --> 00:21:28.437
at a fixed distance as a
function of direction,

293
00:21:28.477 --> 00:21:32.810
normalised to its maximum and
usually in decibels on a polar plot.

294
00:21:32.860 --> 00:21:36.023
The biggest lobe, at 0 dB, is the main

295
00:21:36.063 --> 00:21:39.479
lobe, and all the others
are secondary lobes.

296
00:21:39.529 --> 00:21:42.870
The beamwidth is the angular width
of the main lobe between the

297
00:21:42.910 --> 00:21:47.554
points where it falls to minus 3 dB,
half power, or between its nulls.

298
00:21:47.604 --> 00:21:52.419
The main to secondary lobe level, N L
P S in Catalan, is the difference in

299
00:21:52.459 --> 00:21:57.927
decibels between the main lobe and the
largest secondary lobe, wherever it is.

300
00:21:57.977 --> 00:22:04.248
The front to back ratio compares the main
lobe with the lobe 180 degrees behind it.

301
00:22:04.298 --> 00:22:07.365
Finally, the gain refers to
the power delivered to the

302
00:22:07.405 --> 00:22:10.530
antenna rather than radiated,
so gain equals efficiency

303
00:22:10.570 --> 00:22:13.695
times directivity, practically
equal unless the antenna

304
00:22:13.735 --> 00:22:16.918
is damaged; and the radiation
resistance is the resistor

305
00:22:16.958 --> 00:22:20.588
that would dissipate the same
power the antenna radiates.

306
00:22:22.238 --> 00:22:25.106
Two more antenna parameters
complete the notes, and

307
00:22:25.146 --> 00:22:28.405
both are about connecting
the antenna to a circuit.

308
00:22:28.455 --> 00:22:32.087
The antenna impedance is the ratio
of voltage to current at its

309
00:22:32.127 --> 00:22:37.315
terminals; by reciprocity it is the same
whether the antenna transmits or receives.

310
00:22:37.365 --> 00:22:41.192
To the generator, a transmitting
antenna looks like a resistor that

311
00:22:41.232 --> 00:22:45.795
dissipates the radiated power: that
resistor is the radiation resistance.

312
00:22:45.845 --> 00:22:49.151
The improved model adds
the ohmic loss resistance

313
00:22:49.191 --> 00:22:52.565
of the metal, usually
negligible, and a reactance,

314
00:22:52.605 --> 00:22:56.505
so the impedance is R rad
plus R omega plus j X ant.

315
00:22:56.555 --> 00:23:00.507
When receiving, the same impedance
sits in series with a source, the

316
00:23:00.547 --> 00:23:05.299
open-circuit voltage V ant that the
incident wave induces at the terminals.

317
00:23:05.349 --> 00:23:09.736
The effective length is that open-circuit
voltage divided by the incident

318
00:23:09.776 --> 00:23:14.224
electric field; it has units of metres
but is not the physical length, and

319
00:23:14.264 --> 00:23:18.530
it relates to the effective area
through A ef equals eta, the intrinsic

320
00:23:18.570 --> 00:23:25.227
impedance of free space, about 377 ohms,
times l ef squared over four R ant.

321
00:23:26.877 --> 00:23:29.641
Problem 2.4 gives a measured pattern,

322
00:23:29.681 --> 00:23:32.893
reproduced on the right,
and asks three readings.

323
00:23:32.943 --> 00:23:37.223
The main lobe points east, to 090,
and the minus 3 dB points are

324
00:23:37.263 --> 00:23:41.150
about 65 degrees apart, the
official beamwidth; reading graphs

325
00:23:41.190 --> 00:23:46.029
always has some error, and Barlabé accepts
anything within the reading error.

326
00:23:46.079 --> 00:23:50.762
The largest secondary lobe, here
the back lobe at 270, is at

327
00:23:50.802 --> 00:23:55.368
about minus 18 dB, so the main
to secondary lobe level is 18

328
00:23:55.408 --> 00:23:59.536
dB, which here happens to coincide
with the front to back ratio.

329
00:23:59.586 --> 00:24:02.947
When the antenna turns 30
degrees clockwise, the wave from

330
00:24:02.987 --> 00:24:06.348
the east arrives 30 degrees
away from the main axis, where

331
00:24:06.388 --> 00:24:10.687
the pattern reads about minus 2
dB: the mispointing coefficient.

332
00:24:10.737 --> 00:24:13.933
So 1 microwatt, which is minus 30 dBm,

333
00:24:13.973 --> 00:24:19.458
becomes minus 32 dBm,
which is 0.63 microwatts.

334
00:24:19.508 --> 00:24:23.605
Problem 2.5 is the same with
a formula: sine squared theta

335
00:24:23.645 --> 00:24:27.561
is maximum at theta equal to
90 degrees, and turned 45

336
00:24:27.601 --> 00:24:33.047
degrees it gives sine squared of
45, one half, so 0.5 microwatts.

337
00:24:34.697 --> 00:24:37.044
Now the link-budget
problems, starting with

338
00:24:37.084 --> 00:24:39.877
the two aeronautical
ones of the collection.

339
00:24:39.927 --> 00:24:45.038
In 2.1 the wavelength is 3
times ten to the 8 over 120

340
00:24:45.078 --> 00:24:53.919
MHz, 2.5 m; 5 nmi is 9 260 m;
and 2 dB is a factor 1.585.

341
00:24:53.969 --> 00:24:59.322
Friis gives 10 watts times 1.585
squared times 2.5 over 4 pi

342
00:24:59.362 --> 00:25:05.836
times 9 260, squared, which is 11.6
nanowatts, the official answer.

343
00:25:05.886 --> 00:25:11.694
In 2.2 we solve for the transmitted
power: at 127 MHz the wavelength

344
00:25:11.734 --> 00:25:17.221
is 2.36 m, and the product of
directivities and losses is 1.585

345
00:25:17.261 --> 00:25:24.963
times 1.995 times 0.251, which is
0.794, since minus 6 dB is a quarter.

346
00:25:25.013 --> 00:25:27.643
The minimum transmitted
power is the receiver

347
00:25:27.683 --> 00:25:30.373
sensitivity divided by
that product and by the

348
00:25:30.413 --> 00:25:36.520
free-space factor, which is 1.41
times ten to the minus 11 at 50 km.

349
00:25:36.570 --> 00:25:40.577
That gives 8.9 watts,
again the official answer.

350
00:25:42.227 --> 00:25:45.061
Space links push Friis to the extreme.

351
00:25:45.111 --> 00:25:49.817
In 2.3 we solve for R: R max
equals lambda over 4 pi times the

352
00:25:49.857 --> 00:25:53.947
square root of P T D T D R over
P L min; with lambda equal to 3

353
00:25:53.987 --> 00:25:57.769
centimetres and the product under
the root equal to ten to the

354
00:25:57.809 --> 00:26:04.014
24, the maximum range is 2.39 million
kilometres, the official answer.

355
00:26:04.064 --> 00:26:08.412
In the 2023 partial the distance
is 20 light-minutes, 20 times

356
00:26:08.452 --> 00:26:12.490
60 seconds times the speed of
light, 3.6 times ten to the 11

357
00:26:12.530 --> 00:26:17.371
metres, so the free-space factor
is 4.4 times ten to the minus 29.

358
00:26:17.421 --> 00:26:22.282
The up-link uses the 3 000 watt
ground transmitter, 70 dB, that is

359
00:26:22.322 --> 00:26:26.670
ten to the 7, and the probe's
antenna, with a gain of 50 dB, ten

360
00:26:26.710 --> 00:26:32.132
to the 5, so the probe receives 1.3
times ten to the minus 13 watts.

361
00:26:32.182 --> 00:26:36.722
The down-link uses the same two
antennas but only 300 watts, so the

362
00:26:36.762 --> 00:26:42.703
ground station receives ten times less,
1.3 times ten to the minus 14 watts.

363
00:26:42.753 --> 00:26:46.924
For part c, the effective area is
lambda squared times D over 4 pi,

364
00:26:46.964 --> 00:26:53.120
716 square metres on the ground
and 7.16 on the probe, and if that

365
00:26:53.160 --> 00:27:00.007
is 70 % of the circular aperture,
the diameters are 36 m and 3.6 m.

366
00:27:01.657 --> 00:27:06.361
The 2022 partial asked a
practical version, in decibels.

367
00:27:06.411 --> 00:27:12.147
Add up everything in decibels: 17
dBm transmitted, plus 3 and 3 dB of

368
00:27:12.187 --> 00:27:16.793
directivity, down to a sensitivity
of minus 70 dBm, so the link can

369
00:27:16.833 --> 00:27:23.084
afford 93 dB of free-space loss, which
is 20 log of 4 pi R over lambda.

370
00:27:23.134 --> 00:27:28.620
At 2.4 GHz the wavelength is 12.5
centimetres, so R equals lambda

371
00:27:28.660 --> 00:27:35.466
over 4 pi times ten to the 93 over
20: about 444 metres in free space.

372
00:27:35.516 --> 00:27:39.757
Inside the building, the loss
coefficients are minus 3 dB for

373
00:27:39.797 --> 00:27:43.858
polarization, minus 6 for multipath
and minus 5 for each of the

374
00:27:43.898 --> 00:27:50.158
three walls, a total of minus 24 dB,
which leaves 69 dB for distance.

375
00:27:50.208 --> 00:27:56.630
Then R is only about 28 metres; every
6 dB of extra loss halves the range.

376
00:27:56.680 --> 00:27:59.773
And question 4 of the same
paper is the same rule:

377
00:27:59.813 --> 00:28:03.320
doubling the distance costs
6 dB of signal while the

378
00:28:03.360 --> 00:28:08.558
noise stays the same, so an S
over N of 20 dB becomes 14 dB.

379
00:28:10.208 --> 00:28:13.048
Since you cannot bring anything
into the exam, here is

380
00:28:13.088 --> 00:28:16.317
the sheet to rewrite from
memory on Wednesday evening.

381
00:28:16.367 --> 00:28:19.682
Navigation: ground equals
air plus wind, turn the wind

382
00:28:19.722 --> 00:28:24.281
round by 180, components with
sine and cosine, the sine

383
00:28:24.321 --> 00:28:28.710
and cosine laws, and one minute
of latitude is one nautical mile.

384
00:28:28.760 --> 00:28:33.850
Signals and systems: the mean is c
zero, a cosine gives two lines of A

385
00:28:33.890 --> 00:28:39.347
over 2, rectangle and sinc, the ideal
low-pass impulse response, delta in

386
00:28:39.387 --> 00:28:45.527
gives h out, Y equals X times H, and
non-linear systems create new frequencies.

387
00:28:45.577 --> 00:28:49.958
Noise and data: k T B with T
in kelvin, decibels and dBm,

388
00:28:49.998 --> 00:28:55.353
minus 6 dB per doubling of
distance, regenerative repeaters, 2

389
00:28:55.393 --> 00:29:00.764
B baud, bits per symbol, Shannon,
and more levels mean more errors.

390
00:29:00.814 --> 00:29:04.656
A to D: filter, sampler,
quantizer, coder, sampling at

391
00:29:04.696 --> 00:29:10.150
least at twice W, and 64 kilobits
per second for digital voice.

392
00:29:10.200 --> 00:29:15.516
Antennas: Friis in linear units, the
effective area formula, beamwidth and N

393
00:29:15.556 --> 00:29:20.524
L P S; plus the definitions that are
asked again and again: half-duplex,

394
00:29:20.564 --> 00:29:26.340
broadcast, polarization, loxodrome and
orthodrome, and the network topologies.

395
00:29:27.990 --> 00:29:31.416
Let me finish with a plan for
the days before the exam.

396
00:29:31.466 --> 00:29:34.282
On Sunday, watch parts
one and two again where

397
00:29:34.322 --> 00:29:37.261
needed, and redo the
navigation problems and the

398
00:29:37.301 --> 00:29:41.313
signals problems on paper without
looking at the solutions.

399
00:29:41.363 --> 00:29:45.304
On Monday, part three, the antenna
and data problems, and the list

400
00:29:45.344 --> 00:29:49.736
of theory questions until you can
answer each one in two sentences.

401
00:29:49.786 --> 00:29:54.724
On Tuesday, sit the 2016 and
2022 partials as real mocks, one

402
00:29:54.764 --> 00:29:58.872
hour each, skipping question
5 of 2016, the envelope of a

403
00:29:58.912 --> 00:30:02.546
modulated signal, which is passband
material and out of scope,

404
00:30:02.586 --> 00:30:08.329
and on Wednesday the 2023 paper, then
rewrite the memory sheet from scratch.

405
00:30:08.379 --> 00:30:13.377
On Thursday, a light review in the
morning, then room 2.3 of TR5 at

406
00:30:13.417 --> 00:30:17.756
